 "productionNotes": "The figure almost every source reaches for is 6,939 cars across six model years, and it is quoted by Wikipedia, CorvSport, Vette Vues, classic.com and the Vettes of Atlanta reference. It is not unanimous. Both LSXmag and CorvetteBlogger give 6,922 for the same run, a seventeen-car difference neither side explains. The year-by-year split is shakier still. The set repeated most often is 3,049 (1990), 2,044 (1991), 502 (1992) and 448 in each of 1993, 1994 and 1995, which sums exactly to 6,939 and is given in that form by Vette Vues' own ZR-1 history, Corvette Story and Hagerty. Vette Vues' separate 1995 model-year guide instead says Chevrolet delivered 3,032 units in 1990 - seventeen fewer, and the same seventeen cars that separate 6,922 from 6,939, which hints at a built-versus-delivered distinction rather than a counting error, though no source consulted here says so. CorvetteBlogger attributes 1,344 cars to the 1993 model year alone; 1,344 is precisely the 1993-1995 sum at 448 a year, so it appears to be a three-year total mislabelled. Because the total and the first-year figure both have credible sources on either side, no single production number is asserted here. Two further counts sit outside the run. Eighty-four ZR-1s were assembled to 1989 specification for testing and press use before the launch slipped a year; none were sold new. And the LT5 stopped being built before the car did - Mercury Marine dates the end of LT-5 production to 1994, with Silodrome describing pre-built engines supplying assembly through the final 1995 cars.",
